+</style></head><body><main id="composition" data-start="0" data-composition-id="m3_building-your-future-you-resume-pt1" data-width="1920" data-height="1080" data-fps="30" data-duration="270.07"><section class="clip scene s1" id="beat-01" data-beat-id="beat-01" data-track-index="0" data-start="0.0" data-end="5.45" data-duration="5.45"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 01</div><h1>Building Your Future You Resume</h1><p class="sub">A practical reset for mid-career</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">Hi, and welcome to this lesson on your future-you resume.</p></section><section class="clip scene s2" id="beat-02" data-beat-id="beat-02" data-track-index="0" data-start="5.45" data-end="20.15" data-duration="14.7"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 02</div><h1>An Archive Isn’t a Strategy</h1><p class="sub">Everything you’ve done can hide where you’re going</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">By mid-career, most of us have resumes that have quietly turned into archives — long lists of everything we&#x27;ve ever done. On the surface that looks thorough. In practice, it often works against us.</p></section><section class="clip scene s3" id="beat-03" data-beat-id="beat-03" data-track-index="0" data-start="20.15" data-end="34.02" data-duration="13.87"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 03</div><h1>Tell the Story of What’s Next</h1><p class="sub">Strategic evidence • clear direction • readiness</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">So today, let&#x27;s see your resume differently: not as a historical record, but as a strategic tool that tells the story of where you&#x27;re going next, and why you&#x27;re ready for it.</p></section><section class="clip scene s0" id="beat-04" data-beat-id="beat-04" data-track-index="0" data-start="34.02" data-end="41.7" data-duration="7.68"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 04</div><h1>Why Resumes Miss the Mark</h1><p class="sub">Three common culprits</p><div class="cards"><span>01 TASKS</span><span>02 OUTDATED</span><span>03 NO LINK</span></div><p class="narration">Let&#x27;s start with why so many mid-career resumes don&#x27;t do us justice. There are three usual culprits.</p></section><section class="clip scene s1" id="beat-05" data-beat-id="beat-05" data-track-index="0" data-start="41.7" data-end="60.53" data-duration="18.83"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 05</div><h1>Tasks ≠ Impact</h1><p class="sub">What changed because you were there?</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">First, they&#x27;re overloaded with tasks but light on impact. You&#x27;ll see bullets like &quot;Managed projects,&quot; &quot;Supported faculty,&quot; &quot;Handled student inquiries.&quot; Those describe activity, but they don&#x27;t tell anyone what changed because you were in the role — no results, no scope, no value added.</p></section><section class="clip scene s2" id="beat-06" data-beat-id="beat-06" data-track-index="0" data-start="60.53" data-end="77.3" data-duration="16.77"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 06</div><h1>Busy Isn’t Focused</h1><p class="sub">Update the format. Sharpen the language.</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">Second, the format and language feel outdated or generic. We carry old sections forward and keep phrases we wrote a decade ago, never checking whether the document matches today&#x27;s expectations. The result looks busy but not sharp or focused.</p></section><section class="clip scene s3" id="beat-07" data-beat-id="beat-07" data-track-index="0" data-start="77.3" data-end="101.5" data-duration="24.2"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 07</div><h1>Connect History to Direction</h1><p class="sub">HERE → NEXT</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">Third — and this is the big one for us — there&#x27;s often no clear link between your experience and the role you want next. Someone can read what you&#x27;ve done, but not see how it connects to the step you want next. There&#x27;s no through-line from &quot;here&#x27;s my history&quot; to &quot;here&#x27;s why I&#x27;m ready for this.&quot;</p></section><section class="clip scene s0" id="beat-08" data-beat-id="beat-08" data-track-index="0" data-start="101.5" data-end="112.07" data-duration="10.57"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 08</div><h1>Make the Fit Obvious</h1><p class="sub">Right fit. Right now.</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">All of that makes it harder for hiring managers, sponsors, and decision-makers to say: &quot;Yes, this person is clearly the right fit, right now.&quot;</p></section><section class="clip scene s1" id="beat-09" data-beat-id="beat-09" data-track-index="0" data-start="112.07" data-end="130.07" data-duration="18.0"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 09</div><h1>Build for the Role Ahead</h1><p class="sub">Less proof of busy. More proof of ready.</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">So let&#x27;s flip the perspective. At this stage, your resume is less about proving you&#x27;ve been busy and more about showing you&#x27;re ready for what&#x27;s next. A future-you resume is built around the role you want, not the role you started in.</p></section><section class="clip scene s2" id="beat-10" data-beat-id="beat-10" data-track-index="0" data-start="130.07" data-end="147.25" data-duration="17.18"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 10</div><h1>Curate the Evidence</h1><p class="sub">Projects • Results • Responsibilities</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">Instead of documenting everything, you&#x27;re curating evidence — choosing the projects, results, and responsibilities that best support the direction you&#x27;ve already defined in this course. Every bullet answers one question: &quot;How does this show I&#x27;m prepared for my next move?&quot;</p></section><section class="clip scene s3" id="beat-11" data-beat-id="beat-11" data-track-index="0" data-start="147.25" data-end="171.87" data-duration="24.62"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 11</div><h1>Create a Case File</h1><p class="sub">Future you</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">That might mean highlighting the initiatives where you led at a higher level than your title suggested. Emphasizing outcomes and scope — how many people you impacted, what improved. And de-emphasizing tasks or roles that no longer match where you&#x27;re heading. Your resume becomes less of a timeline and more of a case file for your future you.</p></section><section class="clip scene s0" id="beat-12" data-beat-id="beat-12" data-track-index="0" data-start="171.87" data-end="191.11" data-duration="19.24"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 12</div><h1>Use Your Next Move Statement</h1><p class="sub">Your north star</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">Earlier in this track, you created a Next Move Statement. You named your primary path — promotion, lateral move, or role redesign — and described the role you want in the next 6 to 12 months. In this lesson, that statement is your north star.</p></section><section class="clip scene s1" id="beat-13" data-beat-id="beat-13" data-track-index="0" data-start="191.11" data-end="212.01" data-duration="20.9"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 13</div><h1>Test Every Line</h1><p class="sub">Does this make the case?</p><div class="cards"><span>RESPONSIBILITY</span><span>ACTION</span><span>OUTCOME + SCOPE</span></div><p class="narration">As you update your resume, you&#x27;ll ask of every line: &quot;Does this help make the case for the role in my Next Move Statement?&quot; You&#x27;ll choose three to five experiences that serve as your strongest evidence. And you&#x27;ll rewrite them to clearly show responsibility, action, measurable outcome, and scope.</p></section><section class="clip scene s2" id="beat-14" data-beat-id="beat-14" data-track-index="0" data-start="212.01" data-end="234.15" data-duration="22.14"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 14</div><h1>Show Action, Outcome, Scope</h1><p class="sub">Led 6 • Reduced 30% • Improved 20%</p><div class="compare"><div>Managed team projects</div><div class="strong">Led 6 → onboarding −30% → satisfaction +20%</div></div><p class="narration">For example, instead of &quot;Managed team projects,&quot; you might write: &quot;Led a cross-functional team of 6 to streamline onboarding, reducing time-to-productivity by 30% and improving new-hire satisfaction scores by 20%.&quot; Same person, same work — but the second version paints a much clearer picture of your impact and your readiness for more.</p></section><section class="clip scene s3" id="beat-15" data-beat-id="beat-15" data-track-index="0" data-start="234.15" data-end="256.29" data-duration="22.14"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 15</div><h1>Choose Your Headline Stories</h1><p class="sub">Resume Builder Tool</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">From here, you&#x27;ll use our Resume Builder Tool to upload or paste parts of your current resume, let AI help you draft stronger, impact-focused bullets, and refine the versions that best support your Next Move Statement. You&#x27;ll decide which three to five experiences become your headline stories on your resume and profile.</p></section><section class="clip scene s0" id="beat-16" data-beat-id="beat-16" data-track-index="0" data-start="256.29" data-end="270.07" data-duration="13.78"><div class="eyebrow">MID-CAREER MOMENTUM · 16</div><h1>Build the Resume for What’s Next</h1><p class="sub">Click Next to begin</p><div class="rule"></div><p class="narration">When you&#x27;re ready, click Next to begin working with the Resume Builder Tool, and start reshaping your resume around the role you want, not just the roles you&#x27;ve had.</p></section></main><script src="./assets/vendor/gsap.min.js"></script><script>
